# DuetSoftwareFramework packages from a separately published build of our
# own, installed in place of the Duet3D archive's.
#
# One line per package:
#
#   <package> sha256:<checksum of the published .deb> <https location>
#
# The DSF packages pin each other to one exact version and check it again at
# runtime, so a build of our own is installed as a whole: every package that
# pkg/build.sh --packages=progs,dwc,meta produces in the fork, at a version
# <dsf.version>+<suffix> (3.7.0~rc.2+mp.1 for a modified 3.7.0~rc.2).
# duetwebcontrol is among them: the build packages our fork of Duet Web
# Control, Meltingplot/DuetWebControl, at a version of the same form, and the
# meta package pins it. duetsd and reprapfirmware come from the archive, where
# the build's meta package pins them; reprapfirmware at dsf.rrf_version, which
# the meta package has to accept. An empty list installs the archive's
# packages. Only a published build is taken, checked against the pin: no local
# path, no fallback, a mismatch stops the build. See "Installing a
# DuetSoftwareFramework build of our own" in the README.
#
# A board firmware file is not a package of the build; it goes through
# firmware.list.

# 3.7.0+mp.4: upstream v3.7-dev at 3.7.0, which no longer rejects the skip
# keyword outside G-code files and reads the layer count, the filament used,
# the generator and the print time from the headers of more slicers (the
# filters are in config.json, which the image puts over the printer's copy on
# every boot), with the later upstream fixes for M997 S2, plugins and SPI and
# recover-duet, a tool for root that writes the firmware back to a mainboard
# waiting in its USB bootloader; plus our fix for a DCS crash on a non-finite
# expression result, our fix that keeps the patches coming for a model
# collection the firmware replaces, such as a board's drivers after an M999,
# and the plugin services started together with DCS, so they come back when
# DCS is stopped and started again. The packages are built with root as owner
# of every file. Duet Web Control comes from our fork, 3.7.0+mp.3, the tag
# pkg/dwc-version in the fork pins: upstream 3.7.0 with the CHX 350 operator
# interface, which leaves machine actions such as preheating, homing and
# filament change to the flow macros of the printer configuration, reports a
# flow as completed only when it reaches its `echo ";; Completed"` line, which
# it keeps out of the notifications, forgets a flow answer once its box
# closes, locks the interface while the machine is starting, moves the axes
# while a job is paused, shows the licence notices of both computers and
# history and analysis from the QualityAssurance plugin. The release of that
# Duet Web Control also carries the interface's SBC backend, which
# plugins.list pins at the same version.
# Duet3D has not published 3.7.0 yet, so the meta package accepts the
# firmware of 3.7.0-rc.2, the newest it has published (pkg/firmware-version in
# the fork), which dsf.rrf_version installs. The boards of the CHX 350 run the
# builds in firmware.list either way; from the package the image only uses
# the IAP binary of the mainboard update, which is the same in 3.7.0-rc.1 and
# 3.7.0-rc.2. Earlier builds: mp.3 of 3.7.0 started the plugin services with
# DCS and carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0+mp.2, mp.2 paired the meta package
# with the firmware of 3.7.0-rc.2, mp.1 accepted that of 3.7.0-rc.1, mp.9 of
# 3.7.0-rc.2 brought the root ownership, mp.8 the object model fix, packaged
# as the build user (1001), mp.7 carried Duet Web Control 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.6 and
# the object model changes of upstream, mp.6 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.5, mp.5
# 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.4, mp.4 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.3, mp.3 3.7.0-rc.2+mp.2, mp.9 of
# 3.7.0-rc.1 brought remote sensors on CAN expansion boards, mp.8 checked N
# line numbers in job and macro files, mp.7 RRF-style checksums on numbered
# lines, mp.6 kept the object model in step after an SPI transfer timeout.
